#pagebreak(weak: true)
#align(center, text(size: 7pt, tracking: 0.12em)[QUESTIONS FOR REFLECTION])
#v(0.1in)

#set enum(spacing: 0.9em, indent: 0pt, body-indent: 0.6em)

+ The old man calls the Psalter a garden that holds the whole of Scripture
  in song. Which psalm has carried a story of the Bible for you?

+ Other books we read about other people; the Psalms, he says, we speak as
  our own words. Which psalm have you prayed as if it had been written about
  you?

+ The Psalms are "a kind of mirror." What did you last see of yourself in
  one?

+ Choose one situation from "A psalm for every need" that fits your life
  this week. Pray the psalm he names, slowly, every day.

+ He wants the psalms said "simply, just as it was spoken," without
  improvement. Why might borrowed words pray better than our own?

+ Singing, he says, brings a divided soul into tune. Where are your thoughts,
  desires and actions out of tune with each other?

#pagebreak()
#align(center, text(size: 7pt, tracking: 0.12em)[A SHORT GLOSSARY])
#v(0.1in)
#set par(justify: false, spacing: 0.75em)
#text(size: 7.8pt)[
*Psalter.* The Book of Psalms; also a stringed instrument (the
_psaltery_), which is why the letter can call a person "a psaltery."

*Greek Bible* (Septuagint). The Greek translation of the Old Testament used
by the early Church, from which Athanasius quotes.

*Songs of Ascents.* Psalms 120-134, sung by pilgrims going up to Jerusalem.

*Psalm 151.* A short psalm about David's anointing and his fight with
Goliath, found in the Greek Bible but not in the Hebrew.

*Day of Preparation.* Friday, the day before the Sabbath, the day of the
crucifixion. The "second" and "fourth" days of the week are Monday and
Wednesday.

*Catholic Church.* The universal Church, as against breakaway groups
(schisms) and false teaching (heresies).

*Ahithophel.* David's counsellor, who joined Absalom's revolt (2 Samuel
15-17).

*Ziphites.* People of Ziph who told Saul where David was hiding (1 Samuel 23).

*Sons of Sceva.* Would-be exorcists overpowered by an evil spirit (Acts 19).

*Plectrum.* The pick used to strike the strings of a harp or lyre.
]
#set par(justify: true)
